Project Iris - NASA Earth Data Trend Analysis Made Easy for Everyone
Summary
Iris is a web-based Earth science platform built to democratize the investigation of environmental change. Designed for students, researchers, and curious citizens, Iris bridges the gap between complex satellite telemetry and accessible, evidence-based climate analysis. It empowers users to investigate critical environmental questions- such as whether temperature, rainfall, or vegetation cover is shifting over time in a specific region. The platform features an intuitive, scientifically rigorous workflow using natural language: users select an Earth observation variable, define a geographic region and timeframe, inspect interactive time-series visualizations, and evaluate whether observed shifts represent statistically meaningful trends rather than natural noise. To lower the barrier to entry without sacrificing scientific rigor, Iris pairs educational resources with an investigative workspace. Users can start with the guided “Ask the Detective” interface, which translates plain-language questions into recommended NASA variables and workflows. Inside the workspace, Iris combines visual trend plotting with robust statistical testing, including Mann-Kendall tests, Sen’s slope estimators, and seasonal decomposition, plus AI-assisted dataset discovery. Ultimately, Iris transforms raw, multi-decadal Earth observation datasets into clear, reproducible, and defensible environmental insights, equipping anyone to move from curiosity to validated scientific conclusions.
